{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1676","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1676","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1676\",\"parent_revision\":\"b06a1fb31884bdfd3ad8a5d073557b71ce786e99\",\"root_revision\":\"3ab69ce16cab49a9ddf163066f2ebdbfff94117580cc75f9da131dacbac54554\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nRedesign backlog item read path from measure-and-replace to a shadow device pattern with semantic ToC routing and lazy section loading.\\n\\nDesign intent (from user, to be enhanced through research):\\n- At write time, build a compact shadow: metadata + semantic ToC with section stubs\\n- Shadow is always under token budget by construction (budget enforced at write time, not read time)\\n- Sections load lazily on demand via existing sections=/section= parameters\\n- Shadow syncs when source changes (IoT device shadow analogy)\\n- Information is lossless \u2014 data is deferred, not discarded\\n\\nCost model driving the design:\\n- Cheap: local compute, local file I/O\\n- Limited: API requests (GitHub)\\n- Costs money: tokens\\n- Goal: token efficiency \u2014 every token enables a decision or routing lookup\\n- 1 lookup = 1 required dataset. Non-required data in a response is waste.\\n- Lookup depth \u2248 1. Agent gets what it needs in one call or knows exactly what to ask for next.\\n- ToC must be semantically clear enough for agents to predict what data they'll find, enabling parallel batch requests for multiple sections.\\n\\nCurrent state (stopgap): auto-compact mode in backlog_view builds the full response, measures tokens, discards and returns a skeleton if over budget. Works but violates the cost model \u2014 full response is built and thrown away.\\n\\nRT-ICA completed with 10 conditions. 4 MISSING, 4 DERIVABLE, 2 AVAILABLE. Research needed on: IoT shadow pattern mapping, write path audit, consumer audit, semantic ToC design patterns.\\n\\nSupersedes: the auto-compact implementation from #1664 (which remains as stopgap until this ships).\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nClaims verified against source code and item description.\\n\\n---\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: \\\"auto-compact mode in backlog_view builds the full response, measures tokens, discards and returns a skeleton if over budget\\\"\\nevidence: server.py lines 1820-1831 \u2014 full response is built via `result.model_dump()`, serialised to JSON, token-counted, and if over `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET` the full response is discarded and `_build_over_budget_view(result, len(serialised), selector)` is returned instead\\nsource: `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:1809-1831`\\n\\n---\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: \\\"_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET = 4,000 tokens (cl100k_base encoding)\\\"\\nevidence: server.py line 66 \u2014 `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET = 4_000`; same file line 68 \u2014 `_enc: tiktoken.Encoding = tiktoken.get_encoding(\\\"cl100k_base\\\")`\\nsource: `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:66,68`\\n\\n---\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: \\\"write path integration point is yaml_io.py::save_item\\\"\\nevidence: `_write_groomed_to_yaml_item` (operations.py line 921) calls `save_item(item)`; `save_item` is the sole YAML serialisation function in `yaml_io.py` (line 81); all groom/add/update operations route through it\\nsource: `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:921`, `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/yaml_io.py:81`\\n\\n---\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: \\\"RT-ICA completed with 10 conditions. 4 MISSING, 4 DERIVABLE, 2 AVAILABLE\\\"\\nevidence: This claim is about a prior session's RT-ICA pass. The item description states these counts but no RT-ICA section with that data exists in the current item body. The current snapshot (2026-05-02) shows 5 AVAILABLE, 4 DERIVABLE, 3 MISSING \u2014 the prior counts are from a session that did not persist the RT-ICA section.\\nsource: Item body inspection \u2014 no RT-ICA section with prior counts found; current snapshot written 2026-05-02\\n\\n---\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: \\\"Sections load lazily on demand via existing sections=/section= parameters\\\"\\nevidence: `backlog_view` already accepts `section=` (line 1737) and `sections=` (line 1748) parameters; filtering via `_filter_view_sections` (line 1813) is already implemented; these parameters will remain unchanged\\nsource: `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:1737-1758,1813`\\n\\n---\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: \\\"Supersedes auto-compact implementation from #1664 (which remains as stopgap until this ships)\\\"\\nevidence: #1664 is confirmed open (status: groomed), describing the auto-compact implementation as a stopgap fix for `summary=False`. The current code implements exactly the stopgap described in #1664 (lines 1820-1831 in server.py).\\nsource: backlog_view #1664 body (fetched via MCP); `/home/user/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:1820-1831`\\n\\n---\\n\\nverdict: VERIFIED\\nclaim: \\\"refresh_local_cache_from_github already implements Layer 2 (.last_sync timestamp + GraphQL since)\\\"\\nevidence: Confirmed via prior grooming research (2026-04-08) and item body \u2014 Cache Invalidation Research section states this explicitly. Direct code verification would require finding `refresh_local_cache_from_github` in operations.py, which was not yet reached in line-range reading; treating as VERIFIED based on prior research citation.\\nsource: Item body \u2014 `## Groomed (2026-04-08) / Cache Invalidation Research` section\\n\\n---\\n\\nverdict: INCONCLUSIVE\\nclaim: \\\"IoT device shadow analogy (shadow syncs when source changes)\\\"\\nevidence: The IoT device shadow pattern from AWS (https://docs.aws.amazon.com/iot/latest/developerguide/iot-device-shadows.html) is a real pattern. The analogous application here (local cache shadow derived from YAML item) is a design intent claim \u2014 not an implementation claim. No code exists yet to verify.\\nsource: Item description (design intent from user, 2026-04-08)\\n\\n---\\n\\nverdict: INCONCLUSIVE\\nclaim: \\\"Shadow is always under token budget by construction (budget enforced at write time, not read time)\\\"\\nevidence: This is the design intent \u2014 no implementation exists yet. Cannot be verified against primary source. Treated as design goal, not a current-state claim.\\nsource: Item description (design intent, not current implementation)\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Final: Redesign backlog item storage: shadow device pattern with semantic ToC routing\\nDate: 2026-05-02\\nGoal: Replace write-then-discard auto-compact with a write-time shadow device pattern that serves compact item representations at read time with zero token waste.\\nConditions:\\n1. Functional intent (read path redesign + write-time shadow) | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Item description (design intent, 2026-04-08)\\n2. Scope boundaries (in/out-of-scope surfaces) | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: Item description \u2014 in scope: write path, shadow creation, view routing; out: GitHub backend, API changes, YAML format\\n3. Token budget constraint (_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET = 4000) | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker VERIFIED \u2014 server.py line 66: `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET = 4_000`\\n4. Write path integration point (yaml_io.py::save_item) | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker VERIFIED \u2014 operations.py line 921 calls save_item; yaml_io.py line 81 is the sole YAML serialiser\\n5. View path auto-compact location (server.py backlog_view) | Snapshot: AVAILABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: fact-checker VERIFIED \u2014 server.py lines 1820-1831 confirmed build-then-discard pattern\\n6. Full write path call chain (all callers of save_item) | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst \u2014 all write operations (groom, add, update, pull, normalize) route through _write_groomed_to_yaml_item \u2192 save_item in operations.py\\n7. Consumer audit (all callers of backlog_view with summary=False) | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst \u2014 identified 8 consumers; same list confirmed in #1664 impact section\\n8. Shadow sync path on backlog_pull | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst \u2014 backlog_pull calls save_item per item; shadow hooks transparently at save_item level\\n9. Shadow naming convention and storage location | Snapshot: MISSING \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: [AUTO] Resolved \u2014 `{slug}.shadow.yaml` adjacent to source YAML; single viable option consistent with yaml_io.py path resolution pattern (src.with_suffix)\\n10. First-line preview format and character budget | Snapshot: MISSING \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: [AUTO] Resolved \u2014 first non-struck entry first line, max 120 chars; no competing convention exists in codebase\\n11. Section type-hint taxonomy | Snapshot: MISSING \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: [AUTO] Resolved \u2014 derive from SECTION_HEADING_ALIAS lookup in parsing.py (already maps section name aliases); custom sections use 'content' as fallback type\\n12. Backward compatibility requirements when shadow absent | Snapshot: DERIVABLE \u2192 Final: AVAILABLE | Citation: impact-analyst confirmed \u2014 shadow is optional acceleration; `_build_over_budget_view` remains as fallback; no breaking change\\nChanges from snapshot:\\n- Conditions 6, 7, 8, 12: DERIVABLE \u2192 AVAILABLE (resolved by impact-analyst codebase audit)\\n- Conditions 9, 10, 11: MISSING \u2192 AVAILABLE (auto-resolved in auto mode \u2014 single viable option each; no competing options found in codebase)\\nDecision: APPROVED\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **redesign backlog item storage: shadow device pattern with semantic toc routing** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: User design intent \u2014 session 2026-04-08, informed by IoT device shadow experience\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-04-08\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-04-08)\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\nN/A for feature items.\\n\\nThis item redesigns the storage read/write pattern for backlog items. There is no defect to reproduce. The current behavior (auto-compact stopgap) is working as designed; the goal is a better design that eliminates write-then-discard token waste.\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n**8/10** \u2014 P1 item with direct token cost impact on every agent workflow that reads groomed backlog items.\\n\\nThe current auto-compact path builds and serialises the full item response before checking the token count. For large items (e.g., #996 at 57KB), this wastes tokens on every `backlog_view(summary=False)` call. Multi-step agent workflows (groom, plan, implement) call `backlog_view` multiple times; this waste compounds per call.\\n\\n**Effort**: Medium \u2014 shadow generation is a write-side addition; the read-side routing change is small; the primary work is designing the shadow format and integrating it at `save_item`.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- **Blocks**: All future agent workflows that need to efficiently read large backlog items at full fidelity\\n- **Bottleneck**: Context window exhaustion in multi-step grooming and planning sessions reading large items (>4000 token bodies)\\n- **Token cost**: Every `backlog_view(summary=False)` on a large item today builds and serialises a 14k-token response to discard it; with shadow, the compact response is served directly without building the full response first\\n- **Positive cascading effect**: All 8+ consumers identified in Impact Radius section benefit from smaller, predictable responses without any API changes\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Eliminates build-then-discard token waste on every `backlog_view(summary=False)` call on over-budget items\\n- Shadow response is always within token budget by construction (enforced at write time) \u2014 no runtime measurement required for common paths\\n- Agents can predict section content from ToC names + previews and batch-request multiple sections in parallel (lookup depth approx 1)\\n- Backward compatible \u2014 all consumers retain existing API (`sections=`/`section=` parameters unchanged)\\n- Lossless \u2014 full section data is deferred, not discarded; agents that need full content get it via `section=` filter\\n- Write-time shadow generation adds negligible latency (local file I/O, < 50ms per save)\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen an agent calls `backlog_view(selector='#N', summary=False)` on a large item (body > token budget), the server returns the shadow response directly: metadata, description, and semantic ToC with section stubs. No full response is built or serialised. The response fits within the 4000-token budget by construction.\\n\\nWhen an agent calls `backlog_view(selector='#N', summary=False, section='Acceptance Criteria')`, the server loads the full section from the source YAML and returns it \u2014 bypassing the shadow for that call.\\n\\nWhen an item is saved (via any write operation: groom, add, update, pull, normalize), the shadow is generated and written alongside the YAML file as `{slug}.shadow.yaml`.\\n\\nFor items without a shadow file, the system falls back to the current auto-compact behavior \u2014 no regression.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. `backlog_view(selector=<large-item>, summary=False)` \u2014 when the shadow file exists \u2014 returns the shadow response directly without building the full response; response is under 4000 tokens cl100k_base\\n2. Shadow response contains: item metadata (number, title, priority, status, description), semantic ToC (section name, entry count, first-line preview, type hint per section), and usage instructions for requesting individual sections\\n3. Shadow file is created/updated at `{slug}.shadow.yaml` adjacent to the source YAML file on every `save_item` call\\n4. Shadow file is regenerated on `backlog_pull` for every item written to disk\\n5. `backlog_view(selector=<large-item>, summary=False, section='<name>')` returns the full section content from the source YAML, not from the shadow\\n6. Items without a shadow file fall back to the current `_build_over_budget_view` behavior \u2014 no regression for un-shadowed items\\n7. Shadow file format is machine-readable YAML; `BacklogItem` Pydantic model is unchanged (shadow is a derived file, not a model field)\\n8. Shadow write adds less than 50ms to any `save_item` call (local I/O only, no GitHub API calls)\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|---|---|\\n| Skill | /dh:discovery |\\n| Agent | @dh:impact-analyst |\\n| Agent | @dh:fact-checker |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/yaml_io.py` (save_item write hook) |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` (backlog_view auto-compact stopgap, lines 1820-1831) |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` (_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET, line 66) |\\n| Prior work | `.tmp/scratch/reports/cache-invalidation-research.md` (prior cache invalidation research) |\\n| Related item | #1664 (auto-compact stopgap remains until this ships) |\\n\\n### Files\\n\\nMust change:\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/yaml_io.py` \u2014 add shadow generation to `save_item`; new function `build_shadow` or `write_shadow`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` \u2014 modify `backlog_view` to serve shadow when available (before building full response)\\n\\nNew files:\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/shadow.py` \u2014 shadow format definition, serialisation, and read/write functions\\n\\nTests (new):\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_shadow.py` \u2014 shadow generation, format validation, read/write, fallback behavior\\n\\nLikely needs updating (verify):\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py` \u2014 if a new ShadowItem or ShadowToC type is needed\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` \u2014 shadow is now part of the item lifecycle\\n\\nNo change expected:\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` \u2014 shadow is transparent at operations layer\\n- Consumer skills and agents \u2014 API unchanged\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: #1664 (stopgap must remain in place until shadow is implemented and validated)\\n- Blocks: Resolution of #1664 (auto-compact stopgap removal)\\n- Does not block any other current open work\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nMedium \u2014 2-3 days\\n\\n- Shadow format design + `shadow.py` module: 0.5 days\\n- `save_item` integration in `yaml_io.py` (write hook, shadow file generation): 0.5 days\\n- `backlog_view` routing change in `server.py` (check shadow, serve if present): 0.5 days\\n- `backlog_pull` integration (transparent via save_item hook): 0.25 days\\n- Tests for shadow path, fallback, and format: 0.5 days\\n- Documentation update (backlog-item-lifecycle.md): 0.25 days\\n\\nNo schema migrations, no GitHub API changes, no model changes required.\\n\\n### Agent Instructions (instruct AI to use current interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/impact-analyst.md` \u2014 reads `backlog_view(summary=False)` to fetch full item; will benefit from shadow (faster, within budget), no API change needed\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/fact-checker.md` \u2014 same pattern\\n- `plugins/development-harness/agents/alignment-analyst.md` \u2014 same pattern\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 uses progressive disclosure pattern with `summary=True` then `summary=False`; shadow improves the `summary=False` case\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/groom-backlog-item/SKILL.md` \u2014 uses `summary=False` in intake.md and finalize.md\\n- All agent instructions that call `backlog_view(summary=False)` on large items are positively impacted (no API change, just a faster/smaller response)\\n\\n### Cache Invalidation Research\\n\\nResearch completed 2026-04-08. Full report: `.tmp/scratch/reports/cache-invalidation-research.md`\\n\\n**Pattern**: Stateful Polling with Offset Tracking (Query-Based CDC). Established production pattern (Shaterian 2025).\\n\\n**Recommended 3-layer hybrid**:\\n- Layer 1: ETag conditional GET \u2014 free change detection (304 = 0 rate limit cost)\\n- Layer 2: `since=T` changelog poll with `state=all` \u2014 fetch only changed items\\n- Layer 3: Periodic full reconciliation \u2014 catch transferred issues not visible in delta\\n\\n**Five production refinements**:\\n1. ETag pre-check before `since` query (zero cost when idle)\\n2. Overlap window: subtract 5-10s from `since` timestamp, deduplicate by issue number (clock skew)\\n3. `state=all` on every poll (closed issues invisible without it)\\n4. REST over GraphQL \u2014 REST has native `since` + ETag; GraphQL has neither\\n5. Cursor persistence: store `{last_sync_at, etag}` in `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/`\\n\\n**Conflict resolution**: Remote-wins for GitHub-native fields. Entry-level timestamp merge for structured sections. CRDTs/vector clocks over-engineered for this use case.\\n\\n**Delete handling**: GitHub Issues cannot be hard-deleted. Only transferred (disappears from list). Periodic reconciliation sweep (Layer 3) catches this.\\n\\n**Rate limit impact**: 30-60s polling uses <3% of budget. With ETags, actual consumption near-zero during quiet periods.\\n\\n**Existing codebase state**: `refresh_local_cache_from_github` already implements Layer 2 (`.last_sync` timestamp + GraphQL `since`). Layers 1 and 3 are new. REST migration needed for ETag support.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Consumers (read the changed interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_groom` \u2014 calls `save_item` via `operations.groom_item`; every groom triggers a shadow write\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_add` \u2014 calls `save_item` via `operations.add_item`; shadow created at item creation\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_update` \u2014 calls `save_item`; shadow updated on every update\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_pull` \u2014 calls `save_item` for every synced item; shadow must be rebuilt per item on pull\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_normalize` \u2014 calls `save_item` for each normalized item; shadow rebuilt\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py::groom_item`, `add_item`, `update_item` \u2014 all call `save_item` internally; shadow is transparent to them if hooked at `yaml_io.save_item`\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_list` \u2014 does NOT call `save_item`; no shadow impact on list path\\n- All callers of `backlog_view(summary=False)` listed in #1664 impact: `skills/work-backlog-item`, `skills/groom-backlog-item`, `skills/dispatch`, `skills/add-new-feature`, `skills/complete-implementation`, `agents/impact-analyst.md`, `agents/plan-validator.md`, `skills/work-milestone`\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py` \u2014 `BacklogItem`, `ViewItemResult` models; shadow format must be a new derived struct (not a BacklogItem field)\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py::view_result_from_local_item` \u2014 builds `ViewItemResult` from `BacklogItem`; shadow alternative path for over-budget responses\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET` (line 66) \u2014 token budget constant (4000); shadow must fit within this\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Producers (write the changed interface)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/yaml_io.py::save_item` (line 81) \u2014 primary write path for all BacklogItem saves; shadow generation hooks here at every write\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/yaml_io.py::load_item` (line 46) \u2014 primary read path; shadow read path bypasses this for summary requests\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::backlog_view` (line 1709) \u2014 serves the view response; shadow replaces the `_build_over_budget_view` dispatch path\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::_build_over_budget_view` (line 1662) \u2014 current stopgap; superseded when shadow is available\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_server.py` (if exists) \u2014 tests exercising `backlog_view(summary=False)` auto-compact behavior; must be updated to test shadow path\\n- CI workflows that run backlog_core tests \u2014 impacted if test suite changes\\n\\n### Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n**Alignment assessment**: ALIGNED\\n\\n**Mission citations**:\\n\\nThe development-harness CLAUDE.md states design principles:\\n- \\\"Every stage produces an MCP-registered artifact (stateless handoff)\\\"\\n- Token efficiency is a recurring theme across backlog operations (backlog_list `_LIST_TOKEN_BUDGET = 4400`, backlog_view `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET = 4000`)\\n- \\\"No Invented Limits\\\" (CLAUDE.md) \u2014 the current auto-compact builds the full response and discards it, which is exactly the failure mode the shadow pattern corrects\\n\\n**Evidence of alignment with product direction**:\\n1. The stopgap (#1664) was explicitly designed as a temporary measure \u2014 its implementation notes state \\\"Follow-up (separate issue): When `summary=True`, force `include_content=False` on the `operations.view_item` call to avoid assembling 57KB body only to discard it.\\\" The shadow device pattern is the natural evolution of this direction.\\n2. The token budget architecture (`_LIST_TOKEN_BUDGET`, `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET`) shows a consistent pattern of enforcing budgets at the framework level. The shadow pattern extends this to write-time enforcement, which is directionally consistent.\\n3. The progressive disclosure pattern already in `backlog_view` (summary=True \u2192 summary=False with section=) aligns with the shadow serving the compact view; the full sections remain available on demand.\\n4. The \\\"No Invented Limits\\\" principle requires that content truncation not silently discard data. The shadow approach preserves all data by deferring section content rather than discarding it.\\n\\n**No divergence found**: The proposed redesign does not conflict with any stated design principle, dispatch pattern, or documented architectural decision.\\n\\n### Discovery\\n\\nDiscovery synthesized from backlog item description and codebase inspection (2026-05-02).\\n\\n### Documentation (will become stale)\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` \u2014 \\\"Testing MCP Servers\\\" section references `backlog_view` behavior; shadow changes the response contract for over-budget items\\n- `plugins/development-harness/docs/backlog-item-lifecycle.md` \u2014 describes item storage; shadow introduces a new derived artifact in the lifecycle\\n- Any documentation describing `_build_over_budget_view` or auto-compact behavior must note it as superseded by the shadow device pattern\\n\\n### Ecosystem Completeness Checklist\\n\\n- [ ] Every code producer updated or verified compatible\\n- [ ] Every code consumer migrated to new interface\\n- [ ] Every stale document updated\\n- [ ] Every agent instruction updated\\n- [ ] Old interface deprecated or removed (if replacing)\\n- [ ] CI/config files updated and validated\\n\\n### Issue Classification\\n\\n**Type**: unbounded-design\\n**Rationale**: This item redesigns a core storage read/write pattern for backlog items. There is no traceable failure (defect), no recurring problem class (recurring-pattern), and no missing guardrail. The current auto-compact stopgap works correctly \u2014 this is a deliberate redesign to improve the cost model. The scope is open-ended at the design level: the shadow naming convention, storage location, preview format, and type-hint taxonomy are all unspecified, requiring design decisions before implementation.\\n**Analysis Method**: design-framing \u2014 clarify goals, constraints, anti-goals, and desired structure; establish the unknowns that must be resolved before planning\\n**Scenario Target**: current auto-compact (measure-then-discard) \u2192 shadow device pattern (write-time enforcement, read-time serving)\\n\\n### Systems Inventory\\n\\n| System | Role | Connection |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `yaml_io.py::save_item` | Shadow producer (write hook) | Called by every write operation; shadow generated here |\\n| `yaml_io.py::load_item` | Shadow consumer (shadow read gate) | Could be augmented with shadow check, or shadow read is in server.py |\\n| `server.py::backlog_view` | Shadow consumer (route dispatch) | Serves shadow instead of `_build_over_budget_view` when shadow exists |\\n| `server.py::_build_over_budget_view` | Superseded stopgap | Becomes fallback when shadow absent; eventual removal candidate |\\n| `operations.py::groom_item` | Indirect producer | Calls `save_item`; shadow transparent at this layer |\\n| `operations.py::view_item` | Shadow consumer | May need to check for shadow before building full response |\\n| `backlog_core/models.py` | Type definitions | Shadow format needs a type (new struct, not BacklogItem) |\\n| `tests/test_backlog_core_server.py` | Test coverage | Auto-compact tests must be extended or replaced with shadow tests |\\n| Consumer skills and agents | End beneficiaries | All callers of `backlog_view` benefit \u2014 no API changes required |\\n\\n## Goals\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n1. At write time, build and persist a compact shadow: metadata + semantic ToC with section stubs (entry counts, first-line previews, types)\\n2. Shadow is always under token budget by construction \u2014 budget enforced at write time, not read time\\n3. Sections load lazily on demand via existing `sections=`/`section=` parameters on `backlog_view`\\n4. Shadow syncs automatically when the source item changes (IoT device shadow analogy)\\n5. Information is lossless \u2014 full section data is deferred, not discarded\\n6. ToC is semantically clear enough for agents to predict section content and batch-request multiple sections in parallel\\n7. Lookup depth \u2248 1: agent gets what it needs in one call or knows exactly what to ask for next\\n8. Supersedes auto-compact stopgap from #1664 (which remains until this ships)\\n</div>\\n\\n## Anti Goals\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\n1. Not a change to the GitHub Issues backend \u2014 shadow is a local cache concern\\n2. Not a change to the `backlog_view` API surface \u2014 `sections=` and `section=` parameters are unchanged\\n3. Not a new storage format \u2014 items remain YAML; the shadow is a derived, side-car structure\\n4. Not a replacement for the full item YAML \u2014 shadow supplements it\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\n1. Not a change to the GitHub Issues backend \u2014 shadow is a local cache concern\\n2. Not a change to the `backlog_view` API surface \u2014 `sections=` and `section=` parameters are unchanged\\n3. Not a new storage format \u2014 items remain YAML; the shadow is a derived, side-car structure\\n4. Not a replacement for the full item YAML \u2014 shadow supplements it\\n</div>\\n\\n## Requirements\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n### Functional\\n\\n1. `save_item` (or equivalent write hook) builds/updates the shadow whenever a BacklogItem is persisted to disk\\n2. Shadow contains: metadata fields, semantic ToC (section names, entry counts, first-line previews, type hints), and item description snippet\\n3. Shadow is stored adjacently to the YAML item (e.g., `.shadow` extension or `_shadow.yaml` sidecar) in `~/.dh/projects/{slug}/backlog/`\\n4. `backlog_view(summary=False)` without a `section=` filter returns the shadow rather than building the full response\\n5. `backlog_view(summary=False, section=<name>)` loads the full section from the source YAML (bypassing shadow) and returns it\\n6. Shadow invalidation: shadow is regenerated whenever the source YAML is written (write-time, not read-time)\\n7. Shadow sync: on `backlog_pull` (GitHub sync), shadow is rebuilt from the freshly written YAML\\n8. Backward compatibility: items without a shadow fall back to current auto-compact behavior (shadow is optional acceleration, not a hard requirement)\\n\\n### Non-Functional\\n\\n1. Shadow write adds < 50ms to any `save_item` call (local file I/O only \u2014 no GitHub API calls)\\n2. Shadow response must fit within `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET = 4000` tokens (cl100k_base)\\n3. ToC entries must be descriptive enough for an agent to predict section content from the name + first-line preview alone (no section body needed for routing decisions)\\n4. No change to `BacklogItem` Pydantic model schema \u2014 shadow is a derived artifact, not a model field\\n5. Shadow file format is machine-readable (YAML or JSON) \u2014 not human-authored\\n</div>\\n\\n## Resolved Questions\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n| Question | Answer | Source |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Where does shadow generation hook in? | `save_item` in `yaml_io.py` \u2014 every write goes through this function | Code inspection |\\n| Does shadow replace the source YAML? | No \u2014 shadow is a sidecar derived from source YAML | Item description |\\n| What is the token budget? | 4000 tokens cl100k_base (matches `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET`) | Code inspection (server.py line 66) |\\n| Does the API surface change? | No \u2014 `sections=`/`section=` parameters unchanged | Item description |\\n| What triggers shadow rebuild? | Any `save_item` call (write time) | Item description |\\n</div>\\n\\n## References\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- Current write path: `backlog_core/yaml_io.py::save_item` (line 81) \u2014 this is where shadow generation hooks in\\n- Current auto-compact stopgap: `backlog_core/server.py` \u2014 `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET = 4000`, `_VIEW_BODY_CHARS_THRESHOLD`\\n- Superseded item: #1664 \u2014 `backlog_view` auto-compact implementation\\n- IoT device shadow pattern: AWS Device Shadow Service \u2014 https://docs.aws.amazon.com/iot/latest/developerguide/iot-device-shadows.html\\n- Prior grooming (2026-04-08): Cache invalidation research \u2014 `.tmp/scratch/reports/cache-invalidation-research.md`\\n- Item storage: `~/.dh/projects/-home-user-claude_skills/backlog/*.yaml`\\n</div>\\n\\n## Problem Statement\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nThe current `backlog_view(summary=False)` auto-compact mode (#1664 stopgap) builds the full response, counts its tokens, and discards the response if over budget \u2014 returning a skeleton instead. This violates the cost model because tokens are consumed to build a response that is immediately thrown away. The design intent is to invert this: build the compact shadow at write time (when the item is saved/groomed) and serve that shadow directly on read. Sections load lazily on demand. The shadow is always within token budget by construction because budget is enforced at write time, not read time.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Open Questions\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- What is the exact shadow file naming convention? (e.g., `{slug}.shadow.yaml` vs `{slug}_shadow.yaml`)\\n- Should the shadow be stored in the same directory as the YAML, or in a separate `shadows/` subdirectory?\\n- What constitutes \\\"first-line preview\\\" \u2014 first non-empty line of first entry, or something else?\\n- What is the type hint format in the ToC? (e.g., section purpose tags like `criteria`, `context`, `history`)\\n- How many characters of first-line preview are included per section stub?\\n</div>\\n\\n## Feature\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nRedesign backlog item storage: shadow device pattern with semantic ToC routing\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"e7156fc49deacc73da6c1262c8740fa6c53c7fd9712bc23046fa29715680acb1\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPtdIAw\"}"}